# What the paper concludes {isGroup: true}

[The challenge to Hume stands unanswered]: Severe aphantasia poses a challenge to Hume's
account of abstraction, and none of the empirical or exegetical responses considered has
successfully addressed it.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§5",
     source: "\"We examined potential empirical and exegetical responses to this challenge and argued that none of them has successfully addressed it.\"",
     note: "The paper's central thesis. Its strength is fixed by note 19, recorded separately as a concession: the claim is that a Humean owes an answer, not that the theory is refuted."}
    <+ [Only a challenge, not a refutation]

[Only a challenge, not a refutation]: That aphantasia cannot be accommodated is not by itself
a ground for holding that Hume's theory of abstraction is fundamentally flawed. #conceded
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "n. 19",
     source: "\"We do not make the strong claim that Hume's theory of abstraction is fundamentally flawed on the grounds that it cannot accommodate the ability of severe aphantasics to engage in abstract thought.\"",
     note: "A scope limit the authors set on their own thesis, and the reason the apex claim is worded as a challenge rather than a refutation."}

[The challenge extends beyond Hume]: The existence of severe aphantasia may also pose problems
for other historical and contemporary theories that try to ground higher-level mental phenomena
in the capacity to generate mental imagery.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§5",
     source: "\"The existence of severe aphantasia may also pose problems for other historical and contemporary theories that try to ground various higher-level mental phenomena in the capacity to generate mental imagery.\"",
     note: "The word 'various' is dropped from the claim text; the hedge 'may' is the authors' own and is kept."}
    <+ [The bearing of aphantasia on abstract thought is unexplored]

[Empirical work must distinguish the uses of imagery]: Empirical study of aphantasia should be
sensitive to the difference between possessing mental imagery, using it imaginatively, and using
it in the projective way that Humean abstraction requires.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§1; §4.1",
     source: "\"If our argument is on the right track, empirical study of aphantasia should be sensitive to such nuances\"",
     note: "Compresses the programmatic claim of §1 with its restatement as an invitation to researchers at the end of §4.1."}
    <+ [An invitation to empirical researchers]

[An invitation to empirical researchers]: Empirical researchers should develop more robust
experimental paradigms to test whether aphantasics are capable of projective imagination.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.1",
     source: "\"Our concerns should be read as an invitation for empirical researchers to develop more robust experimental paradigms to test whether aphantasics are capable of projective imagination.\""}

# What Hume's account requires {isGroup: true}

<Hume's mechanism of abstraction>: Hume supplies the psychological mechanism Berkeley lacked,
by which a particular image comes to do general work.
    {note: "Form: interpretive reconstruction of a mechanism, the reading then restated in contemporary terms. The premises are linked: drop the individuality of ideas and the custom has nothing to work on."}

<Hume's mechanism of abstraction>

(1) [All ideas are individual]: Abstract ideas are therefore in themselves individual, however
    they may become general in their representation.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "T 1.1.7.6, quoted §2.2",
     source: "\"Abstract ideas are therefore in themselves individual, however they may become general in their representation.\"",
     note: "Hume's words, adopted by the authors as their reading of him."}
    <+ [Berkeley's starting point]: Hume adopts Berkeley's view that all ideas are individual in
    nature and can only be general by signifying a number of other individual ideas of the same
    kind. #reported
        {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "T 1.1.7.1, §2.2",
         source: "\"Hume's account begins by adopting Berkeley's view that all ideas are individual in nature and can only be general by signifying a number of other individual ideas of the same kind\""}
(2) [Individual ideas are mental imagery]: Hume's individual ideas can be identified with mental
    imagery in the contemporary sense.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2.1",
     source: "\"Individual ideas are literally image-like (T 1.1.7.10; cf. Garrett, 2008: 50) and can be identified with mental imagery in the contemporary sense.\""}
(3) [A general term revives one individual idea]: After a custom of applying one name to
    resembling objects has been acquired, the hearing of that name revives the idea of one of
    these objects, and makes the imagination conceive it with all its particular circumstances
    and proportions.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "T 1.1.7.7, quoted §2.2",
     source: "\"the hearing of that name revives the idea of one of these objects, and makes the imagination conceive it with all its particular circumstances and proportions\"",
     note: "The claim's opening clause paraphrases the sentence before the quoted span, which supplies the custom the revival depends on; the rest is Hume's words."}
(4) [Generality is a readiness to imagine any of them]: What is required is the readiness of the
    mind to imagine any of the individual ideas that fall under the general term.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2.2",
     source: "\"What is required is the readiness of the mind to imagine any of the individual ideas that fall under the general term.\""}
    <+ [The grasp of generality is only partial]: The mind cannot entertain all the ideas a
    general term signifies, so it conjures some of them and holds itself ready to consider any.
        {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "T 1.1.7.7-10, §2.2",
         source: "\"While the mind cannot entertain all the ideas signified by the term ‘triangle,’ it can conjure some of them and is ready to consider any of them.\""}
-----
(5) [Abstraction is projective imagination]: The psychological mechanism responsible for
    abstraction is a kind of projective imagination that involves invoking some determinate
    mental image and using it to call to mind other mental images that belong under the same
    category.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§2.2",
     source: "\"The psychological mechanism responsible for abstraction is a kind of projective imagination that involves invoking some determinate mental image and using it to call to mind other mental images that belong under the same category.\"",
     note: "The authors' own restatement of Hume in contemporary terms, and the premise the whole challenge turns on."}
(6) [Projection requires selective attention and filtering]: Projective imagination requires
    selective attention to the features that generalize across category members while ignoring
    irrelevant ones, and the filtering out of irrelevant images the mind produces spontaneously.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§2.2",
     source: "\"This sensitivity requires selective attention to the features that generalize across category members (e.g. being triangular) while ignoring the features that are not relevant to the category\"",
     note: "Joins that sentence to the one after it, which adds the filtering requirement. Both are needed later, in §4.1, where reduced frontal delta power is said to bear on filtering."}
-----
(7) [Humean abstraction requires imagining with imagery]: Hume's account requires the capacity to
    imagine by using mental imagery, in the specific projective way just described, and not
    merely the possession of imagery.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§2.2; §3",
     source: "\"Recall that for Hume, abstraction requires imagining by using mental images.\""}

[Projective use is more than having imagery]: Using imagination projectively is different from
merely having mental imagery, and is more specific than imagination in the general sense of
controlled manipulation of mental images.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§2.2",
     source: "\"This way of using imagination is thus different from merely having mental imagery.\"",
     note: "The distinction the authors introduce in §1 and then use twice: to rebut the unconscious-imagery response and to draw the methodological moral."}
    +> [Humean abstraction requires imagining with imagery]

## Identifying Hume's ideas with mental imagery

<The identification of ideas with imagery>: Hume's individual ideas and contemporary mental
imagery are the same psychological phenomenon.
    {note: "Form: argument from similarity, backed by an elimination — no other contemporary category is a candidate."}

<The neuroscientific category objection>: Hume's individual ideas and contemporary mental
imagery belong to two different kinds of category, so the two cannot be identified. #reported
    {note: "An objection the authors raise on an opponent's behalf in order to answer it. Form: refutation of an identification by pointing to a difference in kind. Reconstructed rather than sketched because the authors answer it at two different points -- once at a premise and once at the inference."}

<The neuroscientific category objection>

(1) [Mental imagery is a neuroscientific category]: Mental imagery is a neuroscientific category
    that is essentially defined in terms of neurocognitive processes. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2.1",
     source: "\"it could be argued that mental imagery is a neuroscientific category that is essentially defined in terms of neurocognitive processes (compare Nanay, 2023)\"",
     note: "Nanay 2023 is cited as a comparison, not as the objection's author. Deliberately not marked `quotation` although the words are the source's: the hedge 'it could be argued that' sits just outside the span, and this is a view the authors report in order to answer."}
    <- [Imagery is not merely a neuroscientific category]: Even if the causal basis of mental
      imagery consists in neural processes, the phenomenological and representational
      similarities are sufficient to conclude that mental images and Hume's individual ideas
      are identical.
        {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2.1",
         source: "\"Even if its causal basis consists in neural processes, there are still sufficient phenomenological and representational similarities between mental images and Hume's individual ideas to conclude that the two are identical.\""}
(2) [Hume's ideas are phenomenal, not neuroscientific]: Hume's notion of individual ideas has
    nothing to do with neuroscientific categories, and is instead understood in phenomenal and
    representational terms. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2.1",
     source: "\"Hume's notion of individual ideas, on the other hand, has nothing to do with neuroscientific categories, and is instead understood in phenomenal and representational terms.\""}
-----
(3) [Ideas and imagery differ significantly]: There are significant differences between Hume's
    individual ideas and mental imagery. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§2.1",
     source: "\"It could be objected that there are significant differences between them.\""}
    -> [Individual ideas are mental imagery]

<The neuroscientific category objection>
    <_ [The property cluster view licenses the identification]: Even if the causal basis of mental
      imagery is neurophysiological, the homeostatic property cluster theory of natural kinds
      makes its phenomenological and representational features part of understanding the kind,
      and Hume attributed many of those features to individual ideas.
        {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§2.1",
         source: "\"the characteristic phenomenological and representational features of mental imagery are important for understanding it as a natural kind, even if its causal basis is rooted in something neurophysiological or neuropsychological\"",
         note: "An undercut, not a rebuttal: it grants that the essence of mental imagery may lie in something neural and denies that this blocks the identification. The source field carries the general statement of the view; the claim adds the application the authors draw from it two sentences later."}
    <_ <Failure of the identification would be worse for Hume>: Even granting that the two
      categories cannot be identified, that would not save Hume — it would render his account
      of abstraction immediately hopeless from the contemporary perspective.
        {fidelity: "interpretation", warrant: "coherence", pinpoint: "§2.1",
         source: "\"If it turned out that the contemporary category of mental imagery cannot be identified with Hume's notion, this would render his account of abstraction immediately hopeless from the contemporary perspective.\"",
         note: "Read as an undercut rather than a rebuttal: the authors do not deny that the two categories might differ, they deny that conceding the difference protects the Humean. The sentence is stated without any label for its argumentative role, so the role is the reconstruction's reading."}

# The case that resists the account {isGroup: true}

<Aphantasia challenges Humean abstraction>: Severe aphantasics abstract without the capacity
Hume's account makes a precondition of abstraction.
    {note: "Form: refutation by counterexample, deliberately weakened by the authors to an explanatory challenge (note 19). The premises are linked."}

<Aphantasia challenges Humean abstraction>

(1) [Humean abstraction requires imagining with imagery]
(2) [Severe aphantasics cannot imagine using imagery]: Severe aphantasics appear to lack the
    capacity to imagine by using mental imagery.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3; §5",
     source: "\"Since individuals with severe aphantasia appear to lack that capacity while nevertheless engaging in abstract thought, this raises the question of how Hume's account of abstraction can accommodate such cases.\""}
(3) [Severe aphantasics think abstractly]: Aphantasics as cognitively functioning individuals are
    capable of abstract thought as much as anyone else.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3",
     source: "\"aphantasics as cognitively functioning individuals are capable of abstract thought as much as anyone else\""}
    <+ [Aphantasics articulate abstract thoughts]: This is evident in everyday situations and in
    experimental contexts where aphantasics are competent conversational partners who articulate
    their thoughts about abstract matters.
        {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§3",
         source: "\"This is evident both in everyday situations and in experimental contexts where aphantasics are involved as competent conversational partners and articulate their thoughts about abstract matters.\""}
-----
(4) [The explanatory challenge]: Hume's imagistic account of abstraction faces the explanatory
    challenge of accommodating the ability of aphantasics to engage in abstract thought.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3",
     source: "\"Hume's imagistic account of abstraction faces the explanatory challenge of accommodating the ability of aphantasics to engage in abstract thought.\""}

[Severe aphantasics cannot imagine using imagery]
    <- [Aphantasia may be misreport or faulty introspection]: Aphantasia may be due to
      idiosyncratic reporting, or may involve faulty introspection rather than any imagery
      deficit. #reported
        {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§3",
         source: "\"According to the first, aphantasia is due to idiosyncratic reporting. According to the second, aphantasia involves faulty introspection\"",
         note: "Two of the four accounts of aphantasia the paper lists from Phillips 2026. The paper promises to address 'some of the controversies' in the next section and in fact addresses only the unconscious-imagery account, so these two are raised and dropped. Recorded here as an unanswered attack rather than silently completed."}

<Severe aphantasia is a real and cross-modal deficit>

(1) [Severe aphantasics report no imagery]: Severe aphantasics are individuals who, as indicated
    by their self-reports, are unable to form mental imagery, at least insofar as conscious
    imagery is concerned.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3",
     source: "\"Severe aphantasics are individuals who, as indicated by their self-reports, are unable to form mental imagery, at least insofar as conscious imagery is concerned.\""}
(2) [Less subjective measures track aphantasia]: There are neurophysiological and behavioural
    differences associated with aphantasia that do not depend on self-report, such as the reduced
    or absent imagery bias in binocular rivalry.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§3",
     source: "\"There are now also other, less subjective neurophysiological and behavioral differences associated with aphantasia, however.\""}
(3) [Aphantasia is not confined to vision]: Aphantasia is not just a visual imagery impairment
    but concerns other sensory modalities as well.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§3",
     source: "\"aphantasia is not just a visual imagery impairment but concerns other sensory modalities as well\""}
-----
(4) [Severe aphantasics cannot imagine using imagery]

[Severe aphantasia is rare but real]: Roughly 1% of the human population are estimated to have
lifelong severe aphantasia, on some data 4% are aphantasic, and the condition is by now
well-established.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§1; §3",
     source: "\"some data suggest that 4% of the human population are aphantasics (Dance et al., 2022), while people with lifelong severe aphantasia are estimated to make up approximately 1% of the human population\"",
     note: "Recorded because it does opposite work in two places: §3 introduces it as showing the case is worth taking seriously, and §4.4 makes it the premise of the mere-exception response."}

