         note: "The authors' own sentence shows they take the list to be open. Their conclusion is correspondingly modest — that no response HAS succeeded, not that none could — so this undercut bites only against a stronger reading."}

## The three options mentioned in passing

[Involuntary imagery does not help]: Severe aphantasics could not perform Humean abstraction by
using involuntary mental imagery, because in Hume imagination is under at least some control of
the agent.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4",
     source: "\"This proposal is not workable because in Hume, imagination is under at least some control of the agent, i.e., it is not entirely involuntary.\""}
    <- [Aphantasics retain involuntary imagery]: Severe aphantasics have intact involuntary mental
      images, so they could perform Humean abstraction by using involuntary mental imagery.
      #reported
        {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4",
         source: "\"someone could try to argue that since severe aphantasics have intact involuntary mental images (Krempel and Monzel, 2024; K¨onigsmark et al., 2021), they could perform Humean abstraction by using involuntary mental imagery\""}

[Memory does not help]: Severe aphantasics could not perform Humean abstraction by using memory
instead of imagination, because Humean memory preserves the previously experienced order and
position of past experiences, and it is arguably the lack of such constraints that makes
imagination the suitable candidate to play the abstractive role.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4",
     source: "\"in Hume, memory preserves the previously experienced order and position of past experiences and it is arguably the lack of such constraints that makes imagination the suitable candidate to play the abstractive role\""}
    <- [Aphantasics could abstract by remembering]: Severe aphantasics could perform Humean
      abstraction by using memory, instead of imagination. #reported
        {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4",
         source: "\"someone could try to argue that severe aphantasics could perform Humean abstraction by using memory, instead of imagination\""}

[Other sensory modalities do not help]: The proponent of the Humean view is not safe to assume
that aphantasics can perform abstraction by using imagery from other sensory modalities.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4",
     source: "\"the proponent of Humean view of abstraction is not safe to assume that aphantasics can perform abstraction by using imagery from other sensory modalities\""}
    <+ [Aphantasia can be multisensory]: A clustering analysis found a profile of aphantasics who
      relied predominantly on verbal strategies while having low imagery across all sensory
      modalities.
        {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4",
         source: "\"One of the clusters included aphantasics who relied predominantly on verbal strategies while having low imagery across all sensory modalities\""}
    <- [Non-visual imagery may be intact]: Since aphantasia research has focused on visual
      imagery, a case could be made that aphantasics have imagery of other modalities intact and
      could perform abstraction by it. #reported
        {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4",
         source: "\"a case could be made that aphantasics have imagery of other modalities intact, by which they could perform abstraction\""}

## Unconscious imagery

[Unconscious imagery does not help]: In light of the available empirical evidence, it is
implausible that aphantasics can perform Humean abstraction by using unconscious mental imagery.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.1",
     source: "\"it is implausible that aphantasics can perform Humean abstraction by using unconscious mental imagery\""}

<The unconscious imagery response>: Aphantasics lack only conscious imagery, and can perform
abstraction with the unconscious imagery they retain. #reported
    {note: "Form: inference to the best explanation of the behavioural and neural findings. Reconstructed to be attacked at its premise and undercut at its inference — the paper does both, and says so: 'There are two reasons for this.'"}

<The unconscious imagery response>

(1) [Aphantasics have intact unconscious imagery]: Aphantasics do have mental imagery, although
    they are not conscious of it themselves. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.1",
