## Words as proxies

[Words as proxies do not help]: Aphantasics could not perform Humean abstraction by operating
with words as proxies for mental imagery.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     section: "4.3 Abstraction by using words as proxies"}

<The words as proxies response>: Individuals with aphantasia are able to mimic abstraction by
using operations with words as proxies for mental imagery. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     source: "\"individuals with aphantasia are able to mimic abstraction by using operations with words as proxies for mental imagery\"",
     note: "Form: extension of a role the paper has already granted to language in Hume's own account."}

<The words as proxies response>

(1) [General terms already do work in Hume]: General terms invoke individual ideas connected to
    them and enable the mind to regard an individual idea as representing all ideas of its kind.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     source: "\"General terms invoke individual ideas connected to them and enable the mind to regard an individual idea as representing all ideas of its kind.\""}
(2) [Hume allows terms without complete ideas]: Hume states that in reasoning, which is analogous
    to abstracting, we do not annex distinct and compleat ideas to every term we make use of.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "T 1.1.7.14, §4.3",
     source: "\"we do not annex distinct and compleat ideas to every term we make use of\""}
(3) [Aphantasics rely on verbal strategies]: Aphantasics rely on verbal strategies to perform
    various behavioral tasks. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     source: "\"aphantasics rely on verbal strategies to perform various behavioral tasks\""}
-----
(4) [Aphantasics could abstract with words alone]: Aphantasics do not use any ideas or imagery in
    the processes of abstracting and reasoning, relying solely on linguistic operations. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     source: "\"aphantasics do not use any ideas or imagery in the processes of abstracting and reasoning, instead relying solely on linguistic operations\"",
     note: "The authors mark this as the more radical version of the response."}
    -> [Words as proxies do not help]

[Hume's account of thinking is essentially imagistic]: The ideational component is necessary for
forming abstract ideas, so that while the operational use of words is an integral part of
abstraction, the process as Hume describes it is inevitably concerned with individual ideas.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     source: "\"While the operational use of words is an integral part of the process of abstraction, the process, as described by Hume, is inevitably concerned with individual ideas.\""}
    -> [Aphantasics could abstract with words alone]

<Words without ideas would be nonsense>: On the proposal, severe aphantasics would be thinking
and speaking nonsense by Hume's own standard.
    {note: "Form: reductio. Premise 3 is supplied by the reconstruction; without it the passage records an absurdity but draws no conclusion from it."}

<Words without ideas would be nonsense>

(1) [The response makes aphantasics use words they do not understand]: The response suggests that
    individuals with severe aphantasia think and speak using general terms whose meanings they do
    not grasp.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     source: "\"this response suggests that individuals with severe aphantasia think and speak using general terms whose meanings they do not grasp\""}
(2) [Words without ideas are nonsense for Hume]: For Hume, using words without any associated
    ideas would be equivalent to thinking and speaking nonsense.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     source: "\"it seems clear that for him, using words without any associated ideas would be equivalent to thinking and speaking nonsense\""}
(3) [Aphantasics do not speak nonsense]: Severe aphantasics do not think and speak nonsense.
    {fidelity: "imputation", warrant: "enthymeme", pinpoint: "§4.3",
     note: "Never stated in §4.3, and the reductio does not close without it. The authors do assert the corresponding claim about abstract thought in §3, and describe the parallel conclusion in §4.4 as verging on the offensive, so it is plainly theirs."}
