(4) [Words as proxies do not help]

## Aphantasia as a mere exception

[Aphantasia cannot be treated as a mere exception]: Hume could not, given his commitments, treat
severe aphantasia as an exception calling for a different explanation from his basic account.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"we focus on whether Hume, given his commitments, could treat severe aphantasia as an exception that calls for a different explanation from his basic account that appeals to imaginative operations with individual ideas\"",
     note: "The authors' answer to their own question is 'It is highly implausible that he could.'"}

<The mere exception response>: Hume could treat aphantasia as an interesting exception without
giving up his account of abstraction. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"Hume could just treat aphantasia as an interesting exception.\"",
     note: "Form: appeal to precedent, the precedent being Hume's own handling of the missing shade of blue. The authors note this response differs from the rest in letting Hume keep his account as a good explanation of typical cases."}

<The mere exception response>

(1) [Severe aphantasia is rare but real]
(2) [A theory need not explain every case]: From the fact that a theory of abstraction does not
    explain all the cases of abstraction, we should not conclude that the theory isn't fruitful,
    insofar as it still makes sense of most cases. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"From the fact that a theory of abstraction does not explain all the cases of abstraction, we should not conclude that the theory isn't fruitful, insofar as it still makes sense of most cases.\""}
-----
(3) [Hume may keep the account and file aphantasia as an exception]: Hume might not have to treat
    severe aphantasics as constituting a counterexample that would require giving up his account
    of abstraction. #reported
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"Hume might not have to treat them as constituting a counterexample that would require giving up his account of abstraction\""}
    -> [Aphantasia cannot be treated as a mere exception]

<The missing shade of blue precedent>: Hume himself kept the Copy Principle while granting a
counterexample to it, so a Humean may do the same here. #reported
    {note: "Form: argument from precedent, attributed to Earp 2020. Undercut below, not attacked: the authors grant that Hume did this and deny that aphantasia is the same kind of case."}

<The missing shade of blue precedent>

(1) [Hume granted the missing shade as a counterexample]: Hume allows that someone who has seen
    every shade of blue but one could fill in the gap and form an idea of the missing shade
    without a corresponding impression, which seems to violate the Copy Principle. #reported
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "T 1.1.1.10; EHU 2.8, §4.4",
     source: "\"Hume allows that it is conceivable that this person, upon seeing all the other shades arranged in a sequence, could mentally fill in the gap and form an idea of the missing shade, even though they have never had a corresponding sensory impression.\""}
(2) [Hume kept the principle anyway]: Hume treats this as a singular and minor exception, so rare
    and unimportant, in his view, that it does not justify revising or rejecting the general
    principle. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"Hume treats this as a singular and minor exception, so rare and unimportant, in his view, that it does not justify revising or rejecting the general principle\""}
-----
(3) [A Humean may keep a theory and grant an anomaly]: Hume can regard the missing shade of blue
    as a legitimate counterexample yet still retain the broader theory by treating the case as an
    isolated anomaly. #reported
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"Hume can regard the missing shade of blue as a legitimate counterexample yet still retain the broader theory by treating the case as an isolated anomaly\"",
     note: "Attributed by the authors to Earp 2020."}
    +> [A theory need not explain every case]

<The missing shade of blue precedent>
    <_ <The missing shade analogy breaks down>: Even granting that Hume kept the Copy Principle
      through the missing shade of blue, that precedent does not extend to aphantasia, because the
      two cases differ at exactly the point that decides the question.
        {note: "Form: disanalogy — the standard critical question against an argument from precedent, and an undercut rather than a rebuttal."}

<The missing shade analogy breaks down>

(1) [Abstract thought underlies most cognition]: The capacity for abstract conceptual thinking
    underlies most, if not all, cognitive processes.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"the capacity for abstract conceptual thinking underlies most, if not all, cognitive processes\"",
     note: "The authors' hedge 'most, if not all' is kept; it is what makes the disanalogy argument a matter of degree rather than a proof."}
(2) [A special mechanism would make aphantasic cognition wholly different]: Positing a special
    abstraction mechanism for aphantasics would imply that most, if not all, of their cognitive
    mechanisms differ from those of non-aphantasics.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"positing a special abstraction mechanism for aphantasics would imply that most, if not all, of their cognitive mechanisms differ from those of non-aphantasics\""}
(3) [Filling a missing shade underlies nothing]: The capacity needed to fill in a missing
    impression does not underlie cognition in general, so a mechanism for that case carries no
    such consequences.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"the capacity needed to fill in a missing impression does not underlie cognition in general\""}
-----
(4) [Aphantasia is not a minor curiosity]: The missing shade of blue can plausibly be treated as
    an exceptional case so narrow and contained that it poses no serious challenge to the
    theory's explanatory reach, and aphantasia cannot.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"Hume can plausibly treat the phenomenon as a minor curiosity, i.e., an exceptional case so narrow and contained that it poses no serious challenge to the theory's explanatory reach. In contrast, aphantasia raises a more serious concern.\""}
    +> [Aphantasia cannot be treated as a mere exception]

<The Copy Principle leaves no room>: A non-imagistic route to abstraction is not something Hume's
system has space for.
    {note: "Form: reductio against the exception manoeuvre, turning on a foundational commitment rather than on the aphantasia evidence."}

<The Copy Principle leaves no room>

(1) [The Copy Principle is universal]: One of the foundational assumptions in Hume's conception of
    the mind is the Copy Principle according to which all simple ideas are copies of corresponding
    impressions, and it is supposed to be a universal thesis.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"one of the foundational assumptions in Hume's conception of the mind is the Copy Principle according to which all simple ideas are copies of corresponding impressions\"",
     note: "Joined to the later sentence 'Because the Copy Principle is supposed to be a universal thesis', four sentences on, which is what makes the premise do its work here."}
(2) [Non-imagistic abstraction conflicts with the Copy Principle]: Postulating an abstractive
    process that does not presuppose imagistic operation with individual ideas is inconsistent
    with the Copy Principle, which implies that ideas inherit their particular nature from the
    impressions they copy.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"This is inconsistent with the Copy Principle because the latter implies that ideas inherit their particular nature from the impressions that they are copies of.\"",
     note: "Joins two passages about 150 characters apart: the sentence stating what a non-imagistic abstractive process would imply, and the next sentence saying why that is inconsistent with the Copy Principle."}
-----
(3) [Giving up the Copy Principle gives up Hume]: By giving up the Copy Principle, the resulting
    account of the mind would cease to be Hume's account in any meaningful sense.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"By giving up the Copy Principle, the resulting account of the mind would cease to be Hume's account in any meaningful sense.\""}
    -> [Hume may keep the account and file aphantasia as an exception]

<The other horn is offensive>: The alternative to revising the theory is a conclusion about
aphantasics that cannot be accepted.
    {note: "Form: the second horn of a dilemma. The first horn is that the account must be given up, which is what the Copy Principle argument shows Hume cannot afford."}

<The other horn is offensive>

(1) [Standing pat commits Hume to denying aphantasics abstract thought]: If Hume were to remain
    satisfied with his theory of abstraction as it stands, this would commit him to the view that
    individuals with severe aphantasia are incapable of abstract thought.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"if Hume were to remain satisfied with his theory of abstraction as it stands—as he does in the case of the Copy Principle despite the challenge posed by the missing shade of blue—this would commit him to the view that individuals with severe aphantasia are incapable of abstract thought\"",
     note: "The claim text drops the em-dashed aside about the Copy Principle; the source field carries the sentence whole."}
(2) [That conclusion verges on the offensive]: Such a conclusion is not only implausible; it
    verges on the offensive, since it would entail that severe aphantasics should be treated as
    devoid of reason.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§4.4",
     source: "\"Such a conclusion is not only implausible; it verges on the offensive. It would entail that severe aphantasics should be treated as devoid of reason.\""}
-----
(3) [Aphantasia cannot be treated as a mere exception]

# The wider reach {isGroup: true}

<Nothing in the argument is peculiar to Hume>: The argument turns on the imagistic grounding of
thought, not on anything specific to Hume.
    {note: "Form: generalisation from a test case. The authors keep it conditional and hedged, and so does the reconstruction."}

<Nothing in the argument is peculiar to Hume>

(1) [The line of argument is not limited to Hume]: The line of argument we propose is not limited
    to Hume or to the Early Modern period.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "n. 4",
     source: "\"The line of argument we propose is not limited to Hume or to the Early Modern period.\"",
     note: "In the footnote this is the consequent of a conditional: if the critical analysis proves correct, a similar argument is likely against other image-based theories of thought. The conditional, not the consequent alone, is what the authors assert."}
(2) [Prinz's proxytypes use the same mechanism]: The kind of abstraction mechanism that Hume
    appealed to can be found in Jesse Prinz's theory of concepts, so if the argument against the
    Humean account has any bite, it also has it against Prinz's view.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§1",
     source: "\"If the argument in this paper against the Humean account of abstraction has any bite, it also has it against Prinz's view.\"",
     note: "Joins the sentence identifying Hume's abstraction mechanism in Prinz's proxytypes with the conditional drawn from it three sentences later. The authors' conditional form is kept."}
(3) [Grounded theories rest on off-line perceptual representations]: There are theories of
    concepts and language comprehension that are predicated on the manipulation of off-line
    sensory and perceptual representations in grounding conceptual thought, and aphantasics pose
    a prima facie challenge to such theories.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§1",
     source: "\"there are theories in that field that are predicated on the manipulation of off-line sensory and perceptual representations in grounding conceptual thought\"",
     note: "Joins that sentence to the next paragraph's claim that aphantasics pose a prima facie challenge to such theories, about 180 characters later."}
-----
(4) [The challenge extends beyond Hume]

[The bearing of aphantasia on abstract thought is unexplored]: The implications of aphantasia for
understanding the genesis of abstract thought have not yet been explored in contemporary research
on concepts and language comprehension, and this is a significant gap.
    {fidelity: "compression", pinpoint: "§1",
     source: "\"implications of aphantasia on understanding the genesis of abstract thought have not yet been explored in contemporary research on concepts and language comprehension\"",
     note: "The authors hedge this with 'As far as we know'. Kept as a claim about the state of the field, which is what makes the paper's contribution worth making rather than what makes it true."}

<Presence of imagery settles nothing on its own>: What has to be measured is a use of imagery,
not its presence.
    {note: "Form: a methodological moral drawn from the distinction the paper introduces. The premises are linked and read as a chain of insufficiencies."}

<Presence of imagery settles nothing on its own>

(1) [Projective use is more than having imagery]
(2) [Imagery evidence does not show imaginative use]: Evidence for the presence of mental imagery
    does not by itself establish that such imagery is being used imaginatively.
    {fidelity: "quotation", pinpoint: "§1",
     source: "\"evidence for the presence of mental imagery does not by itself establish that such imagery is being used imaginatively\""}
(3) [Imaginative use does not show projective use]: Evidence of imaginative use does not suffice
    to show that imagery is used in the way required for Humean abstraction.
    {fidelity: "paraphrase", pinpoint: "§1",
     source: "\"nor does evidence of imaginative use suffice to show that imagery is used in the way required for Humean abstraction\""}
-----
(4) [Empirical work must distinguish the uses of imagery]
